drafts":[{"headline":"Eight of 10 recent audit recommendations have been fully implemented and closed.","lead":"Strong implementation rates on audit recommendations indicate the council is taking assurance seriously and is responsive to identified control weaknesses, reducing governance risk.","iso":"2026-10-05","paper_title":"Internal Audit Q1 Update 2026/27","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6584/6584.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Internal audit function not in full conformity with external quality assessment standards.","lead":"A governance gap in external audit oversight reduces council control over how its audit function is independently assessed, which could be relevant to peers evaluating controls and to lenders assessing governance risk.","iso":"2026-10-05","paper_title":"Internal Audit Q1 Update 2026/27","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6584/6584.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Four audit recommendations remain overdue due to workload, capacity pressures, unsuccessful recruitment, and system implementation delays.","lead":"Overdue audit recommendations citing capacity and recruitment problems indicate operational constraints that could affect the council's ability to implement other critical changes and suggest underlying staffing or resource issues.","iso":"2026-10-05","paper_title":"Internal Audit Q1 Update 2026/27","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6584/6584.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Audit, Governance and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Q1 Update 2026/27","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6584/6584.pdf","headline":"Eight of 10 recent audit recommendations have been fully implemented and closed.","detail":"Of 10 high and medium priority recommendations reviewed by BDO, eight have been fully implemented and closed. Completed recommendations span Member Enquiries, Corporate Governance, Cyber Security in the Supply Chain, Procurement and Contract Management, and Project Management. Two remain in progress with agreed revised implementation dates. This shows the council is largely responsive to audit findings across a range of governance and operational areas.","category":"audit","matters":"Strong implementation rates on audit recommendations indicate the council is taking assurance seriously and is responsive to identified control weaknesses, reducing governance risk.","figures":"8 of 10 high and medium priority recommendations fully implemented and closed.","quote":"Of these, eight recommendations have been fully implemented and closed, while the remaining recommendations are still being progressed.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Audit, Governance and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Q1 Update 2026/27","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6584/6584.pdf","headline":"Internal audit function not in full conformity with external quality assessment standards.","detail":"The Internal Audit function is in full conformity with eight of nine Global Internal Audit Standards Essential Conditions, but does not fully conform with Standard 8.4 on External Quality Assessment. The council does not approve the scope, frequency, assessor competencies or action plans for external quality assessments conducted by BDO. An alternative arrangement is proposed whereby the Committee will receive information on scope and outcomes of future external assessments.","category":"governance","matters":"A governance gap in external audit oversight reduces council control over how its audit function is independently assessed, which could be relevant to peers evaluating controls and to lenders assessing governance risk.","figures":"8 of 9 Essential Conditions in full conformity; 1 of 9 assessed as 'Generally Conforms'.","quote":"While BDO undergoes an independent External Quality Assessment every five years across its internal audit service, the Council does not directly approve the scope, frequency, assessor competencies or any resulting action plans, as required under the Essential Conditions.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Audit, Governance and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Q1 Update 2026/27","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6584/6584.pdf","headline":"Four audit recommendations remain overdue due to workload, capacity pressures, unsuccessful recruitment, and system implementation delays.","detail":"Of 10 reviewed high and medium priority recommendations, four remain overdue: two from Overtime and Expenses audit citing workload and capacity pressures (revised 31 October 2026), one from Policy Framework audit delayed by unsuccessful recruitment to a permanent post (revised 30 September 2026, though multiple previous date revisions raise credibility concerns), and one from Debt Recovery audit delayed by system implementation prerequisites (anticipated end of Q2 2026/27). The pattern of overdue recommendations indicates operational constraints affecting the council's ability to implement its own audit-directed improvements.","category":"people","matters":"Overdue audit recommendations citing capacity and recruitment problems indicate operational constraints that could affect the council's ability to implement other critical changes and suggest underlying staffing or resource issues.","figures":"Four of 10 reviewed high and medium priority recommendations classified as overdue. Revised implementation dates: 31 October 2026, 30 September 2026, end of Q2 2026/27.","quote":"Two medium-priority recommendations from the Overtime and Expenses audit remain overdue. These relate to the development of an Overtime Policy and a Travel and Subsistence Policy. Both recommendations have experienced delays due to workload and capacity pressures.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Audit, Governance and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Reserve Risk Assessment update report (Audit plan addendum)","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6578/6578.pdf","headline":"The majority of English district councils received disclaimed audit opinions for 2024/25, with assurance gaps in reserves, property plant and equipment, and income and expenditure common across the sector.","detail":"This is a sector-wide issue affecting most district councils, not unique to Rochford. However, Rochford's relative positioning in Quartile 1 indicates the council is handling these sector pressures better than most peers, despite the disclaimer opinion.","category":"governance","matters":"A peer finance director can understand this is a structural sector issue requiring multi-year attention; a lender can see Rochford is managing these common pressures better than most peers, which informs relative credit assessment","figures":"majority of district councils, 2024/25","quote":"The majority of district councils received disclaimed audit opinions for 2024/25, with assurance gaps over reserves, property, plant and equipment, and income and expenditure common across the sector.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-05","committee":"Audit, Governance and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Reserve Risk Assessment update report (Audit plan addendum)","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6578/6578.pdf","headline":"EY will seek a fee variation to cover the cost of reserve risk assessment work and historic assurance rebuilding procedures, outside the scale fee agreed in April 2026.","detail":"The reserve risk assessment, MHCLG submission preparation, and procedures for rebuilding historic assurance fall outside the baseline scale fee set by EY's April 2026 Audit Planning Report. The variation will be negotiated and communicated once agreed. The 2024/25 audit required a fee variation of \u00a328,580, providing a reference point for potential costs.","category":"audit","matters":"Audit fee variations signal expanded scope and additional costs; knowing a prior-year variation was \u00a328,580 helps the council estimate potential 2025/26 costs and indicates the quantum of additional audit work required","figures":"\u00a328,580 (2024/25 variation)","quote":"In addition, procedures relating to the rebuilding of historical assurance will also be outside the scale fee. Accordingly, EY will seek a variation to the scale fee to reflect these changes to the scope of their work","material":false}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-07"},"name":"Rochford","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-26","since_iso":"2026-08-27","distress":false,"headline":"The \u00a3649k forecast surplus is largely driven by non-recurring treasury management benefits that will not persist.","lead":"A lender assessing the council's financial position needs to understand that the apparent Q1 strength masks a weaker underlying operational position; the surplus will reverse as treasury income normalises to budget levels.","also":["2 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Scrutiny on 2026-09-28","n_findings":3,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Q1 Budget Update","top_paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6289/6289.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":90,"news_drafts_total":3}}